Relevant columns and why they matter (only the ones we will actually need)

• event_date : to restrict the three‑month window and to build the year‑month bucket (Nov‑20, Dec‑20, Jan‑21).  
• event_name : to keep only the “purchase” events.  
• user_pseudo_id : stable identifier that lets us find all the events of any customer that ever bought the Google Red Speckled Tee.  
• items         : repeated RECORD that contains one row per product inside each purchase event.  
  – items.item_name      : to find “Google Red Speckled Tee” and every “other” product.  
  – items.quantity       : number of units purchased in that event.  
  – items.item_id, items.item_category(…) etc. : optional helpers if you want to double‑check uniqueness.  

Typical filter / join conditions we will use

• WHERE event_name = 'purchase'  
• AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'  
• UNNEST(items) AS i  
• WHERE LOWER(i.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'            -- to detect the tee  
• WHERE LOWER(i.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'           -- to keep “other” items  
• GROUP BY user_pseudo_id, …                                      -- customer level  
• GROUP BY month, i.item_name                                      -- monthly product counts  
• QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY month ORDER BY qty DESC)=1 -- most purchased

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten exploratory queries (simple ➜ complex ➜ final answer).  
Every query is capped with LIMIT 20 so you can peek at the data.

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at the kinds of events we have
SELECT DISTINCT event_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201109`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek inside the repeated items array for one day in November
SELECT event_date,
       i.item_name,
       i.quantity
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201109`,
     UNNEST(items) AS i
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Do we have the Google Red Speckled Tee on that day?
SELECT DISTINCT i.item_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201109`,
     UNNEST(items) AS i
WHERE LOWER(i.item_name) LIKE '%red%speckled%tee%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Find sample customers (user_pseudo_id) who bought the tee in November
SELECT DISTINCT user_pseudo_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201109`,
     UNNEST(items) AS i
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND LOWER(i.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Show “other” products bought in the same purchase events as the tee (same event_timestamp)
SELECT e.user_pseudo_id,
       i_other.item_name,
       i_other.quantity
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201109` AS e
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
  AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Extend the previous query to the whole three‑month window (all daily tables by wildcard)
SELECT event_date,
       e.user_pseudo_id,
       i_other.item_name,
       i_other.quantity
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_*`  AS e
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'
  AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
  AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate other‑product quantities by customer & month
SELECT SUBSTR(event_date,1,6) AS yrmo,
       e.user_pseudo_id,
       i_other.item_name,
       SUM(i_other.quantity) AS qty
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_*` AS e
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'
  AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
  AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
GROUP BY yrmo, e.user_pseudo_id, i_other.item_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Sum across all customers to see which products were bought the most each month
SELECT SUBSTR(event_date,1,6) AS yrmo,
       i_other.item_name,
       SUM(i_other.quantity) AS qty
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_*` AS e
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'
  AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
  AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
GROUP BY yrmo, i_other.item_name
ORDER BY yrmo, qty DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Pick the single “most purchased other product” for every month
SELECT *
FROM (
      SELECT SUBSTR(event_date,1,6) AS yrmo,
             i_other.item_name,
             SUM(i_other.quantity) AS qty,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY SUBSTR(event_date,1,6) ORDER BY SUM(i_other.quantity) DESC) AS rn
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_*` AS e
           CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
           CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
      WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
        AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'
        AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
        AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
      GROUP BY yrmo, i_other.item_name
) 
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY yrmo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: tee‑buyers’ top other product + total quantity per month (Nov‑20, Dec‑20, Jan‑21)
SELECT CASE SUBSTR(event_date,1,6)
           WHEN '202011' THEN '2020‑11'
           WHEN '202012' THEN '2020‑12'
           WHEN '202101' THEN '2021‑01'
       END                                   AS month,
       i_other.item_name                     AS most_purchased_other_product,
       SUM(i_other.quantity)                 AS total_quantity
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_*` AS e
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_tee
     CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS i_other
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
  AND event_date BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20210131'
  AND LOWER(i_tee.item_name) = 'google red speckled tee'
  AND LOWER(i_other.item_name) <> 'google red speckled tee'
GROUP BY month, i_other.item_name
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY month ORDER BY SUM(i_other.quantity) DESC) = 1
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries move from basic inspection to the exact aggregation you asked for.
